+//! Issue #1084 (proposal 0016 X47, `0016:2587`; the pre-mark rule, `0016:1287-1312`; the write
+//! gate, `0016:1341-1360`) — **reconstruction's committed repair pre-marks its destination,
+//! gates its write, and adopts the pre-mark in the commit that repoints the chunk.**
+//!
+//! Before it, the committed repair wrote the rebuilt fragment, then committed the repoint with a
+//! fresh legacy mark on the **vacated** position only: it never read or touched the mark at its
+//! **destination**, and sent the write with no deadline. These legs drive the repair through the
+//! production fenced control point, `reconcile_step`, with a reconstruction context built as the
+//! deployed loop builds it (`crates/server/src/custodian.rs`, `run_reconstruction_until`: the
+//! context's clock and the pass's `now_millis` one source, the write window `W_WRITE_MILLIS`),
+//! over `RedbMetadataStore::in_memory()` behind a wrapper that can hold or race one commit, and a
+//! placement-aware chunk-store double that enforces `put_fragment`'s deadline on the test's own
+//! clock, as a D server does (`wyrd_traits::WriteDeadlineExpired`). One `ManualClock` backs every
+//! context's clock and every pass's `now_millis`; GC passes run on it too.
+//!
+//! **Topology.** Four D servers, 2–5, in four distinct failure domains. The object — published by
+//! a production multipart Complete, so a second Complete can supersede it — holds one RS(2,1)
+//! chunk on servers 3, 4 and 5 (fragment 2 on server 5), flat or segmented. Server 2 is free.
+//! A repair excludes the survivors' domains, so with server 5 lost server 2 is the only
+//! destination, and with server 2 down server 5 rebuilds in place.
+//!
+//! Each leg runs on a flat and on a segmented object:
+//!
+//! * **(D1)** A stale legacy mark at the destination: once the repair commits, no mark exists at
+//!   any position the new placement names — a fragment rebuilt in place included — and the
+//!   vacated position carries its mark as it always has.
+//! * **(D2)** The round trip: the fragment lost on 5 and rebuilt on 2, then lost on 2, within
+//!   grace, and rebuilt on 5: no mark exists at server 5's position after the second commit.
+//! * **(D3)** The consequence, after (D1) and after (D2): a production Complete supersedes the
+//!   object; GC-only passes with the clock past the old mark's grace reclaim none of its
+//!   fragments before the retirement drain marks them, and every one is gone after those marks'
+//!   grace.
+//! * **(D4)** A destination whose mark is `reclaiming`, or none of the three mark shapes, is
+//!   never written: with no other free domain the repair aborts, names the position on the audit
+//!   seam, and leaves the mark byte-identical. Once GC finishes the reclaim, a later pass repairs.
+//! * **(D5)** GC records its reclaim intent on the destination's mark while the move is in flight
+//!   (a GC pass between the destination write and the move's commit): the move's commit fails, no
+//!   placement names the reclaimed position, and a later pass repairs the chunk.
+//! * **(D6)** A move that loses its commit (the root changes first): the fragment it wrote is
+//!   reclaimed by GC after grace, not kept for ever.
+//! * **(D7)** The write is gated, not only the adoption. Server 2 already holds an unreferenced
+//!   copy of the fragment under an aged mark (an earlier move that lost its commit). **(D7a)** The
+//!   move is held right after its pre-mark lands, the clock passes `W_repoint` and the pre-mark's
+//!   grace, and GC reclaims the old copy and the pre-mark's key: once released, the move sends no
+//!   write at all. **(D7b)** The destination write is held in flight while GC does the same: the D
+//!   server then refuses it, its deadline (the pre-mark's stamp plus the write window) passed.
+//!   After either, no fragment on server 2 lacks both a reference and a mark, the placement is
+//!   unchanged, and a later pass repairs the chunk.
+//!
+//! On the base every leg is red: the destination mark survives (D1, D2), GC reclaims early (D3),
+//! the repair writes onto the `reclaiming` or unreadable destination (D4), the commit lands over a
+//! reclaimed position (D5), the lost move's fragment is kept for ever (D6), no pre-mark is ever
+//! committed (D7a), and the write lands and is adopted after its evidence was reclaimed (D7b).
+//!
+//! The evacuation legs — rebalance's committed move, the same rule — are the test module of
+//! `crates/custodian/src/rebalance.rs`: they build a `RebalanceContext`, whose clock and write
+//! window fields this change adds, so no file here could hold them and compile on the base.
+
+#![forbid(unsafe_code)]
+
